---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Ambience from '../components/Ambience.astro'
import StatusLine from '../components/StatusLine.astro'
import Rail from '../components/Rail.astro'
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro'
import Journey from '../components/sections/Journey.astro'
import InvokeDemo from '../components/sections/InvokeDemo.astro'
import ToolsGrid from '../components/sections/ToolsGrid.astro'
import DocsCards from '../components/sections/DocsCards.astro'
import FooterCta from '../components/sections/FooterCta.astro'

import manifestJson from '../data/manifest.json'
import { parseManifest } from '../lib/manifest.mjs'
import { fetchLatestRelease, resolveRelease } from '../lib/release.mjs'

// Vite resolves this static JSON import relative to this module's own
// source path and inlines it at build time, so Astro's chunk relocation
// under `dist/.prerender/chunks/` (which broke a path computed from
// `manifest.mjs`'s `import.meta.url`, and later a path computed from
// `process.cwd()`) is no longer a hazard by construction — there is no
// runtime path arithmetic left to relocate. The manifest also joins the
// module graph this way, so `astro dev` re-renders when it changes instead
// of serving stale facts from an `fs.readFileSync` Vite can't see.
// `parseManifest` still applies every Task 4 guarantee — required fields,
// the exactly-six-tools check — so a bad manifest fails the build loudly.
const manifest = parseManifest(manifestJson)

// Hero's install command must name this repo, not a hardcoded slug — derive
// it from the manifest's own `repository` URL so a rename breaks the build
// instead of shipping a dead `/plugin marketplace add` command. `repository`
// is `string | null` and not a required manifest field, so guard it with
// `?? ''` rather than assume it is always present.
const repoSlug = (manifest.repository ?? '')
  .replace(/^https?:\/\/github\.com\//, '')
  .replace(/\.git$/, '')
  .replace(/\/$/, '')

// The same slug feeds the release lookup: `fetchLatestRelease`/`resolveRelease`
// each default to a hardcoded `XBlueSky/cc-uplink`, which existed only because
// nothing here used to pass a `repo` argument. A repo rename would otherwise
// keep hitting the old GitHub API path (and therefore the old fallback URL)
// even after Hero's own install command had already picked up the rename —
// two facts on one page quietly disagreeing. `repoSlug || undefined` keeps
// each function's own default live when the manifest has no repository at
// all, rather than handing them an empty string.
const fetchedRelease = await fetchLatestRelease({ repo: repoSlug || undefined })
if (!fetchedRelease) {
  // The GitHub releases API is unauthenticated here (anonymous rate limit is
  // 60/hr/IP, and Pages builders share addresses aggressively), so a fallback
  // is a realistic, recurring event, not just an outage. Surfacing it at
  // build time is the follow-through on `fetchLatestRelease`/`resolveRelease`
  // deliberately not logging inside the leaf module — this page is the
  // caller, so this is where the layering says the warning belongs.
  console.warn(
    'cc-uplink site: could not fetch the latest GitHub release; falling back to the manifest version for the landing page release link.',
  )
}
const release = resolveRelease(fetchedRelease, manifest.version, repoSlug || undefined)
---
<Base title={`cc-uplink — ${manifest.tagline}`} description={manifest.description}>
  <!--
    Pre-paint enhancement gate. Lives HERE (index.astro), not in Base.astro —
    Base is shared with 404/docs pages whose own tests require exactly zero
    scripts, so a gate that every page paid for would break those.

    Deliberately a parser-blocking classic script (`is:inline` — no `src`,
    no `type="module"`, no `defer`/`async`): the HTML parser executes it
    the instant it's reached, synchronously, before the parser continues
    past this point — including before it reaches `[data-page]` below, and
    therefore before first paint. `<script>` (module, whether inlined by
    Astro or chunked externally — see enhance.mjs) is deferred either way,
    which is exactly the bug this replaces: a deep link straight to
    `/#journey` used to paint the flat/static transcript first, then flash
    to the pinned/hidden-lines layout once the module script finally ran.
    This is the ONE evaluation site for the flat/enhanced decision now —
    enhance.mjs no longer re-derives it, it only reads the class back (see
    that file's own gate comment).

    Two classes get stamped here, independently of each other:
      - `enhanced`: motion is allowed (not reduced-motion, and a viewport
        wide enough for the pinned §2/§3 scrubs). Gates every motion-bearing
        rule in the section components.
      - `has-clipboard`: `navigator.clipboard` exists. Gates the copy
        buttons' `display` (see Hero.astro/FooterCta.astro) — not motion, so
        checked unconditionally rather than folded into the `enhanced` gate.

    Both are stamped OPTIMISTICALLY, before any JS has actually run past
    this point — `enhanced` in particular used to double as a promise that
    enhance.mjs's module would go on to wire the scrubs/reveals it gates the
    CSS for. A chunk that 404s, gets blocked, or fails to parse in an old
    browser breaks that promise silently: the class stays, the CSS keeps
    hiding `[data-line]`/`[data-art]`/etc., and nothing is left alive to
    ever add `.on`/`.scrubbing` back — sections pin themselves shut with no
    remaining path to recover.

    The `load` listener below is the self-heal for exactly that failure.
    enhance.mjs's `initEnhance()` sets `<html>`'s `enhanceReady` dataset flag
    as the very first statement of BOTH sides of its own flat/enhanced split
    (see that file) — proof the module actually ran, regardless of which
    side a given visitor landed on, not just that this script optimistically
    guessed it would. Setting it on the flat side too matters just as much
    as the enhanced side: a flat visitor's copy button is already wired by
    the time that split happens, and the heal must not strip `has-clipboard`
    out from under an already-working button just because that visitor never
    qualified for `enhanced` in the first place. A `<script type="module">`
    (deferred, whether Astro inlines it or chunks it externally) always
    finishes running before `load` fires, so on a healthy page — flat or
    enhanced — `enhanceReady` is already set by the time this listener
    checks it and the heal is a no-op. If the module never ran at all, it
    never is, and
    `load` strips both classes back off — the CSS gated on them stops
    applying and the page renders the complete flat experience instead of
    staying pinned half-hidden forever.
  -->
  <script is:inline>
    if (!matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches && innerWidth >= 760)
      document.documentElement.classList.add('enhanced')
    if (navigator.clipboard) document.documentElement.classList.add('has-clipboard')
    addEventListener('load', () => {
      var h = document.documentElement
      if (!h.dataset.enhanceReady) {
        h.classList.remove('enhanced')
        h.classList.remove('has-clipboard')
      }
    })
  </script>
  <Ambience />
  <StatusLine current="home" />
  <Rail />
  <div data-page>
    <Hero
      tagline={manifest.tagline}
      intro={manifest.intro}
      release={release}
      repo={repoSlug}
      pluginName={manifest.name}
      marketplaceName={manifest.marketplace}
    />
    <Journey />
    <InvokeDemo />
    <ToolsGrid tools={manifest.tools} />
    <DocsCards />
    <FooterCta
      release={release}
      repo={repoSlug}
      pluginName={manifest.name}
      marketplaceName={manifest.marketplace}
    />
  </div>

  <!-- The one behaviour module: it self-runs on import, owns the WebGL
       ambience shader bootstrap (constructed unconditionally — the shader
       is the page's background for every visitor), and — only after
       reading the `enhanced` class the gate script above already stamped
       onto <html> pre-paint — wires the §2/§3 scrubs (ambience's own
       progress uniform riding the same shared scroll tick) and everything
       else past that gate. See enhance.mjs's own top-of-file comment. -->
  <script>
    import '../scripts/enhance.mjs'
  </script>
</Base>
